## Lore

The Jackaroo, or Jillaroo, is the Outback character in its purest form.
The word once meant a station hand, Australia''s cowboy, herding cattle and
doing farm work. Stations and herds have come back since the Cataclysm, but
steady work is scarce, so the Jackaroo has grown into a jack-of-all-trades,
equally at home in town, on a ranch or in the bush.

What sets them apart is a cheerful outlook. They have adapted to the new
wilderness instead of fighting to live as people did before, keep to the
woods, grasslands and scrub rather than the deserts, and trade honest services
for what they need: finding water, scouting, guiding travelers through
dangerous country. Unlike a Bushman they still like a hot meal, a roof and
modern gear, and they love to hear and tell a story.

A Jackaroo knows how to survive in the wild, a bit about monsters and magic,
enough Aboriginal lore to stay off sacred ground, how to work animals and fix
things, and how to make friends with nearly anyone. He is handy in a fight,
loves adventure and cares about people.

## GM Notes

Lore: Demons and Monsters is printed at +2%, which may be a misprint for +20%.

Starting kit beyond what the sheet lists: a wide brimmed hat (Jackaroos avoid
goggles and sunglasses), gear webbing, a handkerchief, a durable wristwatch
and a silver plated knife (1D6 S.D.C.); possibly a laser distancer, a
telescopic rifle sight or a hand-held passive nightsight scope. Weapons: a
sturdy S.D.C. rifle (probably bolt-action), a reliable sidearm (maybe an M.D.
pistol) and an M.D. weapon of choice, each with 1D4+1 clips, or 3D4x10 bullets
for a weapon with no magazine.

Transport: a reliable, good quality horse. The character may also own a
beat-up but reliable four-wheel drive M.D.C. vehicle (1D6x10+35 M.D.C.) with
no modifications beyond spare gasoline cans and a winch.

Money: besides the cash, 1D6x$500 in saleable items such as food, seeds, a few
head of cattle or animal skins. Jewelry is worth little in the Outback.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'jackaroo');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'jackaroo';

